"""What each field in the capture window is for, and its shortcuts: the text behind each field's "i" button.

Taken from the browse tool mockup (``capture.js``, ``HELP``), so the two say the same."""

HELP = {
    'task': 'The task this capture belongs to. Hover a task to see its subtasks, and click to pick it, even if it has subtasks. Type to search titles and IDs. Recent lists the last five tasks you saved to. None keeps the capture unfiled, to file later; with New task checked, it makes a top-level task. Typing one #task in the content text picks that task until you pick one here; several #tasks in a note make a capture per task.',
    'types': "What this capture is. Check more than one if it's more than one.\n\nNote: reference information for the picked task, typed or in files, that doesn't change its status. With no task picked, the capture stays unfiled until you file it. New task: creates a task, under the picked one if there is one; the content text becomes its description. Update: a status update to the picked task. It changes its state or another field, or just adds the content text to its status log.\n\nChecking New task or Update unchecks Note, unless you checked Note yourself. Alt+N, Alt+T and Alt+U tick or untick Note, New task and Update. Shortcut: pick a task, start the content text with a state and a period, such as “Closed.”, and Parse and populate checks Update for you.",
    'title': "The new task's name, shown everywhere in DocRepo. A new task needs a title or some content text: if you leave Title empty, Save takes it from the first sentence of the content text. Parse and populate does the same, leaving out lead-ins such as “Remind Tyler tomorrow that he needs to”.",
    'content': "What you type or paste: notes, an email's text or a transcript. Every capture's content text goes into the raw text log of its task. A new task's description is its content text; a new task can be saved without any, as long as it has a title. Drop files or Outlook emails anywhere on the window to add them; a file path or a web link on a line of its own is added too. Expand (F11) makes the content text fill the window, for taking notes in a meeting; F11 again brings the rest back. Formatting, with OneNote's keys: select text and press Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I or Ctrl+U, or use the buttons above the box: strikethrough (Ctrl+-), a yellow, green or pink highlight (Ctrl+Alt+H, or the arrow beside Highlight), and bulleted and numbered lists (Ctrl+. and Ctrl+/, or start a line with “- ”, “* ” or “1. ”). Tab and Shift+Tab, or Alt+Shift+Right and Alt+Shift+Left, move a list item in or out a level. Ctrl+Up and Ctrl+Down go to the paragraph above or below, and Alt+Shift+Up and Alt+Shift+Down move the line, with the items under it. Pasting from OneNote, Word or Outlook keeps bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, highlights and lists, and leaves out fonts, sizes and colours. Parse and populate, titles and search read only the words.\n\nParse and populate (Alt+P) reads the content text to fill the fields below, including a new task's Title: dates (“by next Friday”, “end of October”, “remind him early next week”), people (“remind Tyler”, “assign this to Todd”, “Mike owns it”), labels from their keywords, “urgent”, and repeats (“every Monday”).\n\nQuick update: start with a state and a full stop, colon or dash, such as “Closed. Mike confirmed he doesn't want extra coverage.” Parse and populate sets State to Closed and puts the rest into Current status. Done, Complete and Finished mean Closed; On hold means Parked; Started and Reopened mean In progress.",
    'files': "Files, links and emails added to this capture, one row each. Originals stay where they are. An Outlook email is saved in the capture's folder, and email layer 0 gives a row to each of its attachments, the links in its body and the pictures in it; untick Keep to leave one out. A web or SharePoint link, on a line of its own or from Add link…, is kept as its address only: DocRepo never opens or fetches it, and Copy link on its page copies it.\n\nGive each file a description: what's in it, so you, and later an agent, can tell without opening it. It becomes the document's description. Left empty, it's the task's name, “Attached document” (or link, or picture) and the file's name. Save a copy also copies the file into the task's Files folder, named with today's date. Extract information marks the file for the later processing stage, which reads it into the knowledge base; nothing is extracted yet.\n\nWith several files, “Use the first file's description and choices” gives every row the first row's. Use Add selected Outlook email if dragging from Outlook doesn't work.",
    'agenda': "Puts something on the agenda of a meeting, another task or a person. The agenda item is a link, not a subtask: it stays open, carrying over from meeting to meeting, until it's marked Discussed or removed in the Agendas view.\n\nWith Update, the picked task goes on the agenda and the content text says why. With New task, the new task goes on it. With no task picked, the content text goes on as a note. The task's status log records it. Today, your list for the day, has its own button (Alt+D). Alt+A goes to the picker, which lists meetings first; type to find a person or any task.\n\nA new task with no task picked that goes on Today is a quick task: it waits under Quick tasks, not among your projects, until you put it under its real task (Edit task, E). Ctrl+Shift+D opens the capture window for one.",
    'prompt': "Builds a prompt for an LLM from this capture and copies it, using the summary type you pick. Paste it into Copilot Chat, then paste the answer into Summary. Some types also send the picked task's records. Each type is a file in Config\\prompts that you can edit.",
    'parse': "Shortcut: Alt+P. Fills the fields for the checked capture types from the content text, by fixed rules and with no LLM. What it found is listed beside the content text. For a new task, that includes the Title. With New task and Update both checked, it fills the new task's fields, and for Update only a quick update's state and status. It never overwrites a field you typed in. Filled fields stay highlighted until you edit them, and a list shows the phrase each value came from. Nothing is saved until you press Save.",
    'summary': "The LLM's answer to Copy prompt, pasted here, or a summary you type yourself. Saving adds it to the task's summary log.",
    'labels': "Categories such as Line 1 or Safety. Type to search, or to create a new label; its name becomes its first keyword. Parse and populate adds a label when one of its keywords is in the content text. With Inherit parent labels on (the default), the subtask also gets every parent's labels, shown dashed.",
    'leads': "Who drives the task. Type a name or an alias. Someone not on the people list can be added; they're created when you save. Parse and populate finds leads in “lead: Priya”, “owner - Priya”, “led by Priya”, “Priya owns it” and “I'll lead”. “Remind Tyler” makes you the lead.",
    'assignees': "Who does the work. Parse and populate finds assignees in “assign this to Todd”, “assignees: Mike, Priya”, “remind Tyler”, “Mike to update…”, “Tom and I will…”, “Tom: pull the coupling”, “Tom 20 hours”, “@Tom” and “I'll…”. “Remind him” means the person named just before it. Teams such as Purchasing aren't added as people, and nor is anyone retired.",
    'due': "The soft due date. Lists and days until due use it, and the capacity calendar spreads the hours up to it. Parse and populate reads phrases such as “by next Friday”, “Friday, Oct 16”, “due by end of next week”, “Oct 23”, “Oct 19-23” (with a Start), “in two weeks”, “next month” and “by November”. Fractions such as “3/4 inch” aren't dates.",
    'deadline': 'A hard deadline, or the date of an event. Parse and populate makes a date the Deadline from the words around it: “deadline”, “no later than”, “hard stop” or “at the latest”; else “must”, “ahead of”, “before the…” or an event such as a turnaround, audit or walkthrough. “Due” next to a date keeps it the Due date, as in “Due Friday, deadline Oct 30”.',
    'deadlineDesc': "What the deadline is, such as “Turnaround”. Parse and populate takes the words after “deadline is” or “before”, or else the event's name with the words just before it, such as “Line 2 outage”.",
    'urgent': 'Puts the task on the Urgent list. Parse and populate sets it when the content text says “urgent”, “ASAP”, “immediately”, “right away”, “high priority” or “critical”, but not “not urgent”, “non-critical” or “critical path”.',
    'meeting': 'Makes the task a meeting, one-off or regular (with a Recurrence). Meetings come first when you pick an agenda target, and the Agendas view lists every open meeting. Closing it takes it off those lists.',
    'plantEvent': 'Marks the task as a plant event, such as an outage or a turnaround. The timeline shades it down the whole chart from its Start to its Due, whatever the filters, and the capacity view marks its weeks. Where you pick dates, a line says when they fall inside a plant event. In any capture, **event. makes a line a plant event: **event. Spring outage, 3 to 14 Nov.',
    'references': "Numbers from other systems, such as a change record. Each reference field is named in Settings, and gets a box here, under More fields, and in Edit task. The task's page shows them, with Copy, and Search finds them. Parse and populate fills one when the content text has the field's name followed by a number: for a field named “Work order #”, “work order 48213” or “Work order #48213”.",
    'state': 'Not started, In progress, Parked or Closed. Closed means done or cancelled. Parked tasks are hidden from the task picker and left out of the capacity calendar. Quick update: start the content text with the state and a full stop, colon or dash, such as “Parked. Waiting on budget.” Done, Complete and Finished mean Closed; On hold means Parked; Started and Reopened mean In progress.',
    'status': "A short note on where the task stands now. Each change goes into its status log. Quick update: with “Closed. Mike confirmed he doesn't want extra coverage.”, Parse and populate puts “Mike confirmed he doesn't want extra coverage.” here.",
    'start': 'When the work starts. Today unless you change it. For a routine task, Start and Due set the first occurrence. Parse and populate reads “starting Oct 19”, “12 Oct to 23 Oct”, “Oct 19-23”, “between Oct 12 and Oct 16”, “starting next week” (its Monday) and “every Monday”.',
    'reminder': 'A date to be reminded about the task. Parse and populate takes it from a sentence that asks for a reminder, such as “Remind him early next week”. If nothing else gives a due date, that date is also the Due.',
    'recurrence': "Makes this a routine task, such as a weekly meeting. Each occurrence counts in the capacity calendar. Closing a routine task ends the recurrence. Parse and populate reads “every Monday”, “weekly”, “every other Monday”, “every weekday”, “the second Tuesday of each month”, “monthly” and so on. “At the weekly meeting” names another meeting, so it doesn't count.",
    'items': "In any capture, a line with an operator becomes an item when you save, with a card here for each:\n\n@Tom, or @Tom Becker: a task, with Tom an assignee (several are fine). @L.Tom: a task, with Tom its lead. @__: a task for no one yet. #Rigging plan: a link to that task; with no @ or **, the line is a note on it. A # on a line of its own is a heading: it covers the lines under it, until the next heading. **t or **n: a task or a note, said outright. **Closed., **Done., **Parked., **In progress. or **Started.: an update to the # task with that state, the rest its current status. **u: an update with no state change. **Decision.: a decision, on the # task, else on the capture's task or meeting. **event.: a task that's a plant event, such as **event. Spring outage, 3 to 14 Nov, from its Start to its Due.\n\nA line is a block with the list items under it that have no operator of their own; one with an operator is an item of its own, and a task item listed under another task item becomes its subtask. A task item goes under the capture's task (or the meeting, or the new task) and under each # task; with neither, under Quick tasks. It has no lead without @L. Its title is its first sentence without the operators, and its dates, hours and labels are read as Parse and populate reads them. Type @ or # for a list that narrows as you type.\n\nUntick a card to skip it, or change its title or due date: your change stays even if you edit the line. Each item is saved as a small capture of its own, linked to these notes, so its task's raw text log shows it “from the notes of …”. Editing the notes later doesn't change items already made. ** and a space is never an operator, and # is a link only when it names a task, so “Pump #3” stays text.",
    'meetmode': "Meeting notes (Alt+M) is Expand, plus the meeting's name and its agenda. The task picked at the top decides the meeting: a regular meeting (one with a recurrence) gets this time's meeting as its subtask, named with today's date; a one-off meeting is used as it is; any other task, or none, gets a new meeting under it (top-level with none), named as you type it. The meeting has Meeting ticked and today as its Due, and stays In progress until you close it, so its open follow-ups stay in view. The notes are kept on it, and the items go under it and their # tasks. The draft saves itself every 30 seconds, so a crash or a restart loses nothing; one Save at the end makes everything.",
    'agendaPanel': "The meeting's open agenda items (a regular meeting's own). Start from the agenda puts each in the notes: a task as a # heading, to write under, and a note on its own as an “Agenda:” line. An item is ticked for you once its heading has notes under it, or an item names its task; tick or untick any by hand. Ticked items close as Discussed when you save, and their tasks' status logs say so.",
    'repeatUntil': 'The last date the routine task repeats. Leave it empty to repeat with no end; the capacity calendar then plans five years ahead.',
    'hours': "Hours of work for each person in the role: a total for a one-time task, or per occurrence for a routine one. Every lead gets the full lead hours, and every assignee the full assignee hours. They fill the capacity calendar.\n\nParse and populate reads “6 hours of work”, “about 2.5 hrs”, “90 minutes”, “2 days of work” (8 hours a day), “2-3 hours” (the higher figure), “lead hours: 4” and rates such as “an hour a week”. A rate becomes a total from Start to Due, or over its own length (“4 hours a week for 3 weeks”): an hour a week for 9 weeks is 9 hours. Times such as “in 2 hours” and “a 4 hour outage” aren't work. “For lead”, or the lead's name, in the same sentence makes them Lead hours (with both names, the one just before the hours); otherwise they're Assignee hours.",
}
